<TopicPage slug="subjects/art-history/expert/art-historiography-and-method">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Historiography of art is the <strong>history lesson\'s "history of history" applied to art</strong>:
    every generation of art historians wrote with assumptions their successors exposed — and the
    discipline\'s self-corrections (feminist, postcolonial, visual-culture) are among the
    humanities\' clearest examples of method reforming itself.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="Vasari, the German methods, the critical turns, the expanded field" widget="equationStep" config={histCfg} />

  <h2>Doing art history at the expert level</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Every attribution carries a method:</strong> connoisseurship (Morelli: ears and fingernails — the habits that betray a hand), documentary (archives, contracts), technical (the next lesson\'s science) — the discipline triangulates like the statistics lesson\'s convergent evidence.</li>
    <li><strong>The period eye (Baxandall):</strong> what 15th-century Florentines saw in a painting (sermons, dance, gauging) that we cannot — historical viewing as recoverable skill: the anthropology lesson\'s emic reconstruction, art-historically.</li>
    <li><strong>Foucault and the museum (Bourdieu\'s Distinction):</strong> taste as class marker, the museum as cultural capital\'s bank — the sociology lesson\'s Bourdieu, curated; why attendance demographics are a scholarly datum.</li>
    <li><strong>Writing art history well:</strong> the argument structure (a claim about meaning, evidence from form, context, documents) is the critique lesson\'s order at research scale — and the profession\'s best writing (Gombrich, Clark, Alpers) is craft worth imitating.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Vasari (1550); Nochlin (1971); Baxandall, Painting and Experience; Preziosi, The Art of Art History (anthology).</p>
</TopicPage>
